The difference is sub-microsecond precision: `...41.928761Z` vs `...41.928761300Z`. H2 TIMESTAMP default precision is 6 digits (microseconds); the stored value truncates to micros, while the first response returned the in-memory `sealedAt` (nanosecond precision from Instant.now). So the first sealer returns un-truncated instant; a concurrent sealer reads back the truncated stored value.

Fix: in `seal()`, after markSealed, return the stored value — or truncate to micros. Cleanest: after marking sealed, re-read the batch row and return stored sealed_at, so all responses (first and repeats) are identical. Alternatively truncate sealedAt to microseconds before storing: `Instant.now(clock).truncatedTo(ChronoUnit.MICROS)`. H2 TIMESTAMP precision is 6 (microseconds) by default. Truncating to micros makes in-memory match stored. But safer to re-read from DB. I'll re-read after update within the same tx:

```java
batchRepo.markSealed(batchId, sealedAt);
